{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: remove the inherited UI npm advisory and make future high-severity npm advisories visible in the existing Security workflow.\n\nHugh asked Planner to check Gitseq CI for issues that should be addressed. Current main 869702241e4d28c9b8599e8caccaf3d01a2ca4d9 passes CI34446823882 and Security34446823983, but the npm ci step reports one high-severity vulnerability. A fresh npm audit --prefix ui --package-lock-only --json exits1 for nanoid3.3.17, GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8 / CVE-2026-67213. The registry and [GitHub advisory](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8) identify3.3.18 as a patched compatible3.x release; registry metadata confirms it is available.\n\nThe lockfile's [nanoid entry](ui/package-lock.json@869702241e4d28c9b8599e8caccaf3d01a2ca4d9:1795) is dev-only, reached through [PostCSS](ui/package-lock.json@869702241e4d28c9b8599e8caccaf3d01a2ca4d9:1847), whose ^3.3.17 range admits the patch. npm audit with --omit=dev returns0 and no advisories. The inspected local PostCSS call uses nanoid/non-secure with fixed size6; ui/src has no nanoid/custom-generator use. No deployed denial-of-service path or exploit is demonstrated. This is warranted dependency maintenance and scanner coverage, not a claim that the new S1 head introduced a vulnerability.\n\nThe failure history matters: review5c13c707 and reviewc9fb3555, among other earlier records, called the same advisory inherited/outside their own diff. A full sequence-text search found those disclosures but no nanoid/npm-advisory remediation request. Close that gap now. The [Security workflow](.github/workflows/security.yml@869702241e4d28c9b8599e8caccaf3d01a2ca4d9:24) currently checks Go advisories and secrets, but has no npm audit job. The separate workflow already runs on pushes, PRs and a weekly schedule; preserve that arrangement.\n\nConditions of satisfaction:\n1. Resolve nanoid to the smallest compatible patched version through the current dependency graph, ordinarily3.3.18. Keep unrelated dependency versions unchanged unless a documented resolver requirement makes an additional change necessary. Do not run a broad forced audit fix or an unrelated major upgrade.\n2. Add a bounded npm lockfile advisory check, including development/build dependencies, to the existing Security workflow. Fail that job on high or critical advisories; retain visible diagnostics for lower severities and registry failures. Use the existing Node24/pinned-action conventions, read-only permissions and explicit timeout. Avoid installing or executing project dependencies merely to audit the lockfile. Keep advisory scanning separate from the main CI workflow; do not change branch-protection settings or silently suppress this advisory.\n3. Show before/after audit evidence: current lock exits1 for the named advisory; corrected lock passes the high-severity threshold, with production/development scope explicit. Verify the graph change and UI tests, TypeScript/build and committed-embed reproducibility. Run applicable ciguard tests and required repository checks; use a focused command/fixture check to show the audit failure status cannot be swallowed. No flaky permanent test depending on a live vulnerability database is required.\n4. Independent exact-head Architecture/Security/Simplification review, requester ratification, sealed exact-path merge, push main/sequence/receipt refs, successful CI and Security including the npm job, then remove the owned worktree and branch. Record actual bundle changes if rebuilding requires them. Update behavior documentation only where its existing claims change, with correct artifact provenance.\n\nSuggested branch request/ui-npm-advisory. Treat this as a small next-priority repair at a safe boundary in your existing work; it does not cancel I7, P2 or other commitments. If existing work prevents that, answer with an explicit scheduling disposition rather than assuming the green CI status means nothing is owed. This request owns the npm maintenance/coverage outcome only. The previously failed Markdown-link runs are already repaired, and the latest main race/UI/reproducibility/clone-build checks are green.\n\nCurrent-basis replacement for #22836 (2a7eed7b), requested by Codex in #22841 (0456488e). Codex has confirmed availability at a saved I7 boundary and accepted these unchanged conditions; requester, performer, destination and governing authority are unchanged. This request rests on the live security-policy artifact a5432843: its SECURITY.md blob is identical at current main 86970224 and states that security fixes are made on current main. Native artifact queries found no live exact-path artifact for ui/package-lock.json or .github/workflows/security.yml; their complete immutable current-head source snapshots are attached as direct evidence, alongside the before-audit and CI warning. No historical review is used as a current behavioral basis. Those disclosures and the replaced request remain background evidence only. This is the same maintenance and scanner-coverage outcome, with no new policy adoption or change to the existing review, merge, or branch-protection authority.","body":{"conditions":"Remove GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8 from the UI lockfile with the smallest compatible update and add npm advisory checking, including dev dependencies, to the existing separate Security workflow with high/critical failure and visible diagnostics. Preserve unrelated dependencies, permissions, branch protection and full workflow authority. Verify before/after audit, UI tests/build/embed, ciguard and required checks; independent exact-head review/ratification, sealed merge, push, CI+Security/npm green and owned cleanup.","head":"869702241e4d28c9b8599e8caccaf3d01a2ca4d9","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}